According to library documentation, `res.redirect()` sends a redirect to a URL derived from the given path. It accepts an optional status code, which defaults to `302` (“Found”). It supports:

- fully qualified URLs, e.g. `res.redirect('https://google.com')`
- root-relative paths, e.g. `res.redirect('/admin')`
- path-relative redirects, e.g. `res.redirect('post/new')` or `res.redirect('..')`

Examples from library documentation:
```js
res.redirect('/foo/bar');
res.redirect('http://example.com');
res.redirect(301, 'http://example.com');
res.redirect('../login');
```

The documentation also says that `res.redirect(url, status)` was removed in Express 5; the order is now `res.redirect(status, url)` for explicit status codes. For security, library documentation advises validating user-supplied URLs before redirecting to avoid open redirects.
